 * The script forces `DESK_ADAPTERS=demo`, `DESK_CRM=hubspot`, `DESK_OUTBOX=smtp_provider` and, unless you pass `--live-model`, `DESK_QUALIFIER=recorded`, whatever the file says; it also reads the other `DESK_*` names (for example `DESK_PORT`, the `DESK_HUBSPOT_*`, `DESK_SMTPP_*` and `DESK_LIVE_*` names) from the same file and gives them to the desk processes. Defaults it sets when absent: `DESK_SEND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=8`, `DESK_HUBSPOT_ALLOW_ANY_BASE_URL=no`, `DESK_LIVE_ALLOW_ANY_BASE_URL=no`.
-* Mailboxes: blocks `REAL_IMAP_1_*`, `REAL_IMAP_2_*` and so on are read in order and stop at the first n that has no `REAL_IMAP_n_USER`; the user of block n must be the n-th address of `DESK_SMTPP_RECIPIENTS`. Per block: `USER` and `PASSWORD_FILE` (required), `HOST` (default: the mail provider's address, `MAIL_IMAP_DEFAULT_HOST` in `constants.py`), `PORT` (default 993), `CA_FILE`, `FOLDERS` (comma list; default `INBOX` and the provider's English spam folder, `MAIL_SPAM_FOLDER` in `constants.py`).
-* Exit codes of `scripts/run_real_check.py`: 0 every check in its table passed (with `--cleanup`: and every contact it made was archived; without `--cleanup` the contacts stay where the run put them, in HubSpot for a real run and in the local stand-in CRM that `--dry-fakes` starts for the run and stops at its end, and the exit code does not count them); 1 stopped before calling anything (you did not answer `y`, or there was no terminal to answer on); an unexpected error after that question also ends the program with code 1 (a Python traceback); 2 refused at the start (a missing or bad setting, or `--assume-yes` without `--dry-fakes`); 3 the run could not finish, a check failed, the book could not be read, or (only with `--cleanup`) some contacts could not be archived and were left behind.
+* Mailboxes: blocks `REAL_IMAP_1_*`, `REAL_IMAP_2_*` and so on are read in order and stop at the first n that has no `REAL_IMAP_n_USER`; the user of block n must be the n-th address of `DESK_SMTPP_RECIPIENTS`. Per block: `USER` and `PASSWORD_FILE` (required), `HOST` (the mail server; required: the script never falls back to the provider's address `MAIL_IMAP_DEFAULT_HOST` in `constants.py`, because a key goes there), `PORT` (default 993), `CA_FILE`, `FOLDERS` (comma list; default `INBOX` and the provider's English spam folder, `MAIL_SPAM_FOLDER` in `constants.py`).
+* Exit codes of `scripts/run_real_check.py`: 0 every check in its table passed (with `--cleanup`: and every contact it made was archived; without `--cleanup` the contacts stay where the run put them, in HubSpot for a real run and in the local stand-in CRM that `--dry-fakes` starts for the run and stops at its end, and the exit code does not count them); 1 stopped before calling anything (you did not answer `y`, or the terminal closed before you could); 2 includes "standard input is not a terminal": the y/N question needs a person, so a pipe, a file or a scheduled job is refused before anything starts (the only scripted use is `--assume-yes` together with `--dry-fakes`, which uses local fakes); an unexpected error after that question also ends the program with code 1 (a Python traceback); 2 refused at the start (a missing or bad setting, or `--assume-yes` without `--dry-fakes`); 3 the run could not finish, a check failed, the book could not be read, or (only with `--cleanup`) some contacts could not be archived and were left behind.
+* Addresses a key goes to are never filled in by a default in the check scripts. `scripts/run_real_check.py` refuses (exit 2, `refused: <NAME>: not named in the settings ...`) when `DESK_HUBSPOT_BASE_URL`, any `REAL_IMAP_n_HOST` or, with `--live-model`, `DESK_LIVE_BASE_URL` is missing or blank, unless the default itself would be this computer; name the vendor's own address on purpose or a loopback address for a test. `DESK_SMTPP_HOST` is already required. `scripts/run_live_check.py` has no default `--base-url` and refuses without one. Both scripts' y/N question needs an interactive terminal: with standard input from a pipe, a file or a scheduled job they refuse (exit 2) and start nothing; `run_live_check.py --assume-yes` is accepted only with a loopback `--base-url`. `scripts/hubspot_probe.py` has no question; it needs `--yes-spend-calls N` and a token from the environment, and always talks to the vendor's address unless `--dry-run` names a loopback stub.
 * `scripts/n8n_api.py` reads `N8N_URL` (default `http://127.0.0.1:5679`) and `N8N_API_KEY_FILE` (the path of a one-line file with the n8n API key; required). `N8N_URL` must be an `https` address, or `http` to this computer (any IPv4 address that starts with `127`, `::1`, an IPv4 loopback address written in the `::ffff:` form, or `localhost`, which is exactly what the desk's own `is_loopback_host` accepts): the key is sent in a header, so the script refuses any other address, and one it cannot read, at start (before the key file is opened) with a message that says to use https or an SSH tunnel to `127.0.0.1`. The script also does not follow a redirect from n8n (the key header would go to the new address); it reports the redirect as an error. For `http` to this computer the script ignores the proxy settings of the environment (`HTTP_PROXY`, `no_proxy`): the key header goes straight to this computer and never to a proxy; for `https` those settings are used as usual. Every failure that can come from the address, the key file, the workflow file, the network or n8n's answer ends with a short message and exit code 1 or 2 (never a Python traceback, the key or the body of an answer): n8n not reachable, an answer that is not JSON or is nested too deeply to read, a workflow list with an odd entry, a missing argument, a workflow file that cannot be read (missing, not JSON, nested too deeply, no name), a missing, unreadable or directory key file. A workflow name that the terminal cannot show is printed with `?` in place of the characters it cannot encode. Not covered, so a traceback is possible: Ctrl-C, running out of memory and a closed output pipe. A key file that is empty, longer than 4096 characters, has more than one line, or holds a space, a control character or a character outside printable ASCII is refused at start with a fixed message (the key text is never printed). Network and protocol faults (a bad address, a cut-off answer, a reset) print only the name of the error type, never the address or a header. The address is also refused when it holds a user name part (`@`) or a space, tab or line break, and unless the text between `//` and the first `/` is exactly a host name (letters, digits, dots, hyphens) or a bracketed IPv6 address, optionally followed by one `:port` with a port from 1 to 65535 (so a backslash, a second port, text after a bracket, an empty port and `:0` are refused, and a non-ASCII host name must be given in its ASCII form). n8n's own names such as `N8N_PORT` are n8n's, not this repository's; they appear in `docs/n8n-recipe.md`. `scripts/run_live_check.py` takes command-line flags, not settings names: `--key-file`, `--base-url`, `--ledger`, `--cap-run`, `--cap-total`, `--price-date` and one `--model ID=IN_PRICE,OUT_PRICE` per model, and optionally `--max-tokens`, `--effort`, `--timeout` (see the text at the top of the file).
 * `tools/scrub_n8n_export.py` (cleans an n8n export before it is published; no settings) replaces every address with a scheme (`://`, also with JSON-escaped slashes `:\/\/`), every `data:` link that has a media type, every `javascript:` or `vbscript:` link and every e-mail address that is not `example.test`, `example.com` or `example.invalid`. One kept exception: a web address (`http`, `https`, `ws`, `wss`) to `localhost` or `127.0.0.1` stays, up to the end of its path; its query string and fragment (`?...`, `#...`) are dropped, because an API key can sit in a query. An address with any other scheme to `localhost` (`file://localhost/...`, `postgres://localhost/...`) is replaced whole. A tab, a carriage return or a line feed right after a `localhost` host (a browser ignores it) turns the address into a look-alike: the address, the break and the next run of non-blank characters are replaced whole. Any other blank right after a `localhost` host (a space, a vertical tab, a form feed, a no-break space or another Unicode space; a browser keeps it in the user name) does the same, but only when the next run of non-blank characters holds an at-sign before any `/`, `?` or `#` (`http://localhost @host`); `http://localhost:5678/x y` and `http://localhost y` (words after a space) are not touched. Webhook ids (the id field) are never kept, but the webhook path is kept as written, so a secret inside a path stays. **Not cleaned** (read the output by eye before you publish it): an address without a scheme (`//host/path`, `host:5432`), a bare host name, a path that is not a URL, the scheme written with percent signs (`http%3A%2F%2F`) or with backslashes (`http:\\host`); a secret inside the path of a kept `localhost` address (`/webhook/<token>`), also as a `%3F`-encoded query inside the path (only a real `?` or `#` ends the path); a data: link without a media type (`data:,text`, `data:;base64,...`); an e-mail address written with the small at-sign U+FE6B or with an ideographic full stop in the domain; the keys of a dictionary inside node parameters (only the values are cleaned); and, in a node name, an expression that refers to another node by an old name when two names became the same (the second gets ` (2)`). Over-cleaning by design: an address that an expression completes, such as `"http://localhost:5678/x?" + $json.q`, is read as a plain address and loses its `?` and what follows on the same run of characters. Likewise a bare local address, a space and then an e-mail address (`http://localhost jane@example.test`) loses the e-mail address too.
 * `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N` and `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_RETRY` are read only by one optional measurement test (`tests/test_server_hardening.py`, `test_measure_burst`): with `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N=<number of simultaneous connections>` it fires that burst at a desk it starts itself and prints the status counts (`LEAD_DESK_LOAD_RETRY=1` lets the senders retry a refused connection); without `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N` the test is skipped.
